course: dictionary
lesson: 860
title: One word, two worlds
level: primary-5
stars: 0
explain: Some words belong to more than one subject. Bicarbonate is one of them. In the kitchen, it means bicarbonate of soda, the white powder that makes cakes rise. In science, it means a chemical salt. To know which meaning you need, read the clues around the word.
explain: «Baking soda (bicarbonate of soda) is a mild alkali; it tastes bitter and soapy, not sour.» The brackets tell you that bicarbonate of soda and baking soda are the same thing. Words like cake, mixture or oven point to the kitchen meaning. Words like chemical, acid or blood point to the science meaning.
explain: Ask yourself: what word would fit here? Then try your meaning in the sentence. If it still makes sense, you have probably found the right one.
fact: Bicarbonate is a noun.
fact: Bicarbonate of soda is also called baking soda or sodium bicarbonate.
fact: In cooking, bicarbonate of soda makes cakes rise because it gives off carbon dioxide bubbles.
fact: In science, bicarbonate is a chemical salt containing the group HCO3.
fact: In the body, bicarbonate helps keep blood at the right acid level.
exercise: «Add the bicarbonate to the cake mixture and it will rise.» What is bicarbonate here? | A type of cake tin | A sweet, sticky sauce | A sour juice | *A white powder used in cooking
exercise: «The chemistry teacher said bicarbonate helps keep blood at the right acid level.» What is bicarbonate here? | A kind of bread | *A chemical salt | A white powder for baking | A cleaning spray
exercise: «Baking soda is a mild alkali; it tastes bitter and soapy, not sour.» What does the sentence say baking soda tastes like? | *Bitter and soapy | Sour and fizzy | Salty, like the sea | Sweet, like sugar
test: «Bicarbonate of soda» and «baking soda» are used in the same sentence. What do the brackets show? | A mistake in the text | Two different chemicals | *Two names for the same powder | A recipe with two steps
test: Why does a cake rise when bicarbonate is used? | Because it makes the oven hotter | Because it is a sour fruit | *Because it gives off carbon dioxide bubbles | Because it turns into sugar
exam: Priya reads «bicarbonate» in a science book about blood. Which meaning fits, and why? | The baking powder for cakes, because it is always used in cakes | A cleaning spray, because it removes smells | *The chemical salt, because the clues are blood and acid level | A sour fruit, because the book is about food
